# The `∗`-regular closure is generated by the co-restricted homomorphism

Let `φ : S →+* P` be a `∗`-homomorphism into a `∗`-regular ring `P`, put

`R = starSubringClosure (Set.range φ)` and `A = starRegularClosure R ⊆ P` (so `A` is a `∗`-regular
`∗`-subring of `P`), and let `closureCorestrict φ : S →+* A` be `φ` with its codomain restricted
to `A`.  This file proves that the image of `closureCorestrict φ` generates `A` as a `∗`-regular
`∗`-subring of itself, i.e.

`starRegularClosure (starSubringClosure (Set.range (closureCorestrict φ))) = ⊤`.

Equivalently, `A = R(φ(S), A)` is generated by the image of `φ` as a `∗`-regular `∗`-subring of
itself.  This is the hypothesis needed to apply `isEpic_of_starRegularClosure` to
`closureCorestrict φ` ([JZLA20, Proposition 2.7]).

The mathematical content is the comparison of the two approximating chains of [JZLA20,
Proposition 2.2]: the chain of `R` computed inside `P`, and the chain of
`starSubringClosure (Set.range (closureCorestrict φ))` computed inside `A`.  Since `A` is
`∗`-regular, a relative inverse in `P` of an element of `A` again lies in `A` and is the relative
inverse computed inside `A`; this makes the two chains match stage by stage, while `A` itself is
the `∗`-subring generated by the union of the first chain.
-/
